# hex0 -- turn commented hex into bytes. Rung 0 of the ladder.
# Invocation: hex0 IN OUT
# Platform: x86-64 Linux. Syscalls only. No libc.
#
# The one binary not built from source: the seed, written as commented hex
# so every byte can be audited by hand. The contract is the rung's
# (ladder/0-hex0/README.md and tests/). The machine code and the syscalls
# belong to this target, x86_64-linux. Its source is
# ladder/0-hex0/x86_64-linux/hex0.hex0. Anyone verifies the binary by
# decoding that source with tools/check/hex-check.py and comparing the
# bytes, or by running this seed on that source and comparing again.
# A ladder is the sequence of programs that builds watc, each one built by
# the one below it. A rung is one program in that sequence. The design is
# docs/excursus/2026/10/001-the-ladder/DESIGN-the-ladder.md.
#
# Input, one byte at a time:
#   '#' or ';' starts a comment that runs to the next LF or end of input.
#   A CR does not end a comment.
#   Whitespace (exactly space, tab, CR, LF) is skipped.
#   A byte's two digits may be split by whitespace or a comment.
#   0-9, a-f and A-F are hex digits, two per output byte, high nibble first.
# Output: OUT is created or truncated with mode 0755, so it runs directly.
# An existing OUT is truncated only after fchmod. fchmod runs before
# ftruncate so a failed chmod leaves the previous bytes in place.
# Once OUT has been opened and the checks before fchmod have passed, its
# mode is 0755 on every later status.
# If IN and OUT are the same file (same device and inode, hard link or
# symlink included) and OUT opened, nothing is changed and the status is 7.
# A read-only same file does not open, so the status is 3 and the file is
# unchanged. A device or FIFO fails S_ISREG before fchmod, status 3,
# unchanged. st_mode is the 32-bit field at offset 24. S_IFMT is 0xF000
# and S_IFREG is 0x8000.
#
# What this program does not cover: a signal (SIGXFSZ under ulimit -f)
# ends hex0 with no status, and a FIFO or terminal IN can block.
#
# Total means every failure stops with a named status. hex0 does not report
# done after a failure. What OUT holds:
#   status 1: OUT was not created.
#   status 2: OUT was not created when opening IN failed. When fstat on IN
#     failed, OUT has been opened and not truncated: a new file is empty,
#     and an existing file still holds its old bytes and its old mode.
#   status 3: OUT was not created when the open failed. When fstat on OUT
#     failed, or fchmod failed, or OUT is not a regular file, OUT is not
#     truncated: a new file is empty, and an existing file or device still
#     holds what it held, mode included.
#   status 4: truncated, then the bytes decoded before the bad byte, mode 0755.
#   status 5: truncated, then the bytes decoded before the trailing nibble, mode 0755.
#   status 6: a failed truncate leaves the old bytes, and the mode is
#     already 0755. A failed read or write leaves the bytes written before
#     that failure, after the truncate, mode 0755. A failed close leaves
#     the full decoded bytes, mode 0755.
#   status 7: untouched. This status applies only once OUT opened.
#   status 0: the decoded bytes, mode 0755.
#
# Exit status:
#   0  done
#   1  wrong argument count
#   2  IN cannot be opened, or fstat on IN failed
#   3  OUT cannot be opened, or is not a regular file, or fchmod failed, or fstat on OUT failed
#   4  a byte that is not a digit, a comment, or whitespace (exactly space, tab, CR, LF)
#   5  an odd number of digits at end of input
#   6  a read, write, close or truncate failed
#   7  IN and OUT are the same file, and OUT opened
#
# Instruction comments are AT&T syntax. Each begins with the file offset of
# that instruction. Every jump target in an instruction comment is an offset
# from the first code byte (file offset 0x78, virtual 0x400078). objdump of
# the code alone starts at 0, and those addresses match it.
# The three exit entry points are status 4 (push 4), die (pop the status
# into edi), and exit (syscall 60). Success jumps to exit with edi = 0.
# The read loop, the comment-byte handler, and the status-4 push are marked
# in the block headings.
# syscall returns in rax and clobbers rcx and r11. Every other register
# survives, so the loop keeps rsi as the one-byte buffer and rdx as 1.
# rcx is not held across a syscall. rbp holds IN's st_dev and r8 holds IN's
# st_ino. st_dev sits at offset 0 of the stat buffer and st_ino at offset 8.
# p_filesz is the sum 0x78 + 417 = 537.
#
# Registers:
#   r12   fd of IN
#   r13   fd of OUT
#   r14b  1 when a high nibble is waiting, else 0
#   r15b  that high nibble
#   r10b  1 while skipping a comment
#   rbp   st_dev of IN
#   r8    st_ino of IN
#   rcx   clobbered by syscall
#   [rsp] the one-byte buffer, inside the 144-byte stack slot
# The slot is 144 bytes because that is the x86-64 ABI's struct stat.
# fstat is passed rsp, and the write stays inside the slot.
# Syscalls: read 0, write 1, open 2, close 3, fstat 5, exit 60,
#   ftruncate 77, fchmod 91.
# Open OUT with O_WRONLY|O_CREAT (0x41), no O_TRUNC, and mode 0755 (0x1ED).
# Then fstat both descriptors. Then, if OUT is a regular file,
# fchmod(OUT, 0755) and ftruncate(OUT, 0).

# ## ELF header (64 bytes)
7F 45 4C 46             # magic: 7F 'E' 'L' 'F'
02 01 01 00             # 64-bit, little-endian, ELF version 1, System V ABI
00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 # ABI version + padding
02 00                   # e_type: ET_EXEC
3E 00                   # e_machine: x86-64
01 00 00 00             # e_version
78 00 40 00 00 00 00 00 # e_entry: 0x400078, the first instruction
40 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 # e_phoff: 64
00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 # e_shoff: no section headers
00 00 00 00             # e_flags
40 00                   # e_ehsize: 64
38 00                   # e_phentsize: 56
01 00                   # e_phnum: 1
00 00 00 00 00 00       # e_shentsize, e_shnum, e_shstrndx: none

# ## Program header (56 bytes): load the whole file, read and execute
01 00 00 00             # p_type: PT_LOAD
05 00 00 00             # p_flags: PF_R + PF_X
00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 # p_offset: 0
00 00 40 00 00 00 00 00 # p_vaddr: 0x400000
00 00 40 00 00 00 00 00 # p_paddr
19 02 00 00 00 00 00 00 # p_filesz: 0x78 + 417 = 537
19 02 00 00 00 00 00 00 # p_memsz: 0x78 + 417 = 537
00 10 00 00 00 00 00 00 # p_align: 4096

